\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {1}Introduction}{1}{section.1}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces  Motivation of semantic routing in RouteSAM. Existing direct supervision transfer attempts to propagate segmentation information directly from a labeled Anchor to a Target, where a large semantic distance may limit propagation quality. RouteSAM exploits semantic continuity within abundant unlabeled data and introduces intermediate Bridge samples to construct a gradual semantic transition: $A \rightarrow B_1 \rightarrow B_2 \rightarrow \cdots  \rightarrow T$. The unlabeled images serve as semantic bridges that progressively reduce the gap between labeled supervision sources and target images. }}{2}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:motivation}{{1}{2}{Motivation of semantic routing in RouteSAM. Existing direct supervision transfer attempts to propagate segmentation information directly from a labeled Anchor to a Target, where a large semantic distance may limit propagation quality. RouteSAM exploits semantic continuity within abundant unlabeled data and introduces intermediate Bridge samples to construct a gradual semantic transition: $A \rightarrow B_1 \rightarrow B_2 \rightarrow \cdots \rightarrow T$. The unlabeled images serve as semantic bridges that progressively reduce the gap between labeled supervision sources and target images}{figure.1}{}}
